><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20251110100638+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20251110100650+10'00'" name="a57045d9-0dde-4f57-a6d5-96ceb7f8151d" page="6" coords="322.809387,214.670242,560.421814,214.670242,322.809387,204.723129,560.421814,204.723129,322.809387,202.707932,560.439697,202.707932,322.809387,192.760818,560.439697,192.760818,322.809387,190.745621,364.964355,190.745621,322.809387,180.798508,364.964355,180.798508,372.697906,190.745621,387.273315,190.745621,372.697906,180.798508,387.273315,180.798508,395.034302,190.745621,423.995789,190.745621,395.034302,180.798508,423.995789,180.798508,431.714203,190.745621,482.936493,190.745621,431.714203,180.798508,482.936493,180.798508,490.617004,190.745621,500.857697,190.745621,490.617004,180.798508,500.857697,180.798508,508.588379,190.745621,560.416382,190.745621,508.588379,180.798508,560.416382,180.798508,322.809387,178.783310,560.446472,178.783310,322.809387,168.836197,560.446472,168.836197,322.809387,166.820999,560.410400,166.820999,322.809387,156.873886,560.410400,156.873886,322.809387,154.858688,560.429565,154.858688,322.809387,144.911575,560.429565,144.911575,322.809387,142.896378,560.403870,142.896378,322.809387,132.949265,560.403870,132.949265,322.809387,130.990860,560.404175,130.990860,322.809387,121.043747,560.404175,121.043747,322.809387,119.028549,560.428467,119.028549,322.809387,109.081436,560.428467,109.081436,322.809387,107.066238,560.416321,107.066238,322.809387,97.119125,560.416321,97.119125,322.809387,95.103928,560.426147,95.103928,322.809387,85.156815,560.426147,85.156815,322.809387,83.141617,560.445984,83.141617,322.809387,73.194504,560.445984,73.194504,322.809387,71.179306,560.418884,71.179306,322.809387,61.232193,560.418884,61.232193" rect="322.498535,60.921341,560.757324,214.981094" subject="Comment on Text" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Coaches, nutritionists, and researchers are frequently interested&#xD;in the impact of specific training strategies, supplementation,&#xD;and specific interventions on physiological&#xD;responses and performance in combat sport-specific actions,&#xD;specifically combat performance. In this context, reliability&#xD;and sensitivity measures can help these professionals to&#xD;understand whether a real change has occurred or whether&#xD;some bias can explain the variation observed when performance&#xD;before and after a given intervention is compared. In&#xD;this study, judo match simulation, which is commonly used&#xD;during the training process and in investigations to assess&#xD;athletes’ performance, was analyzed. The main results indicated&#xD;that time-motion and physiological responses to this </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>task have large to very large reliability, but most of the variables&#xD;present low sensitivity, because only moderate to large&#xD;changes can be identified. Among the variables investigated,&#xD;peak blood lactate, delta lactate, and time in tachi-waza were&#xD;the most useful variables to monitor changes across judo&#xD;matches, suggesting that these variables can be used to monitor&#xD;changes in physiological and judo-specific technical&#xD;responses.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
